  "answer_text": "**Ukrania** (most commonly spelled **Ukraine** in English, and *Ucrania* in Spanish) is a country located in Eastern Europe. It is the second-largest country in Europe by land area (after Russia), and the largest located entirely within the European continent. \n\n### 1. Key Facts\n* **Capital and Largest City:** Kyiv\n* **Official Language:** Ukrainian\n* **Government:** Unitary semi-presidential republic\n* **Currency:** Hryvnia (UAH)\n* **Borders:** Bounded by Russia to the east and northeast; Belarus to the north; Poland, Slovakia, and Hungary to the west; Romania and Moldova to the southwest; and the Black Sea and Sea of Azov to the south.\n\n### 2. Geography and Economy\nHistorically known as the \"breadbasket of Europe\" due to its incredibly fertile black soil (*chernozem*), Ukraine is one of the world's top agricultural exporters, producing massive quantities of grain, corn, and sunflower oil. \n\nThe country is mostly characterized by fertile plains (steppes) and plateaus, with the Carpathian Mountains situated in the west. Major urban centers and industrial hubs include Kyiv, Kharkiv, Odesa, Dnipro, Donetsk, and Lviv.\n\n### 3. Modern History\nUkraine gained its independence in 1991 following the collapse of the Soviet Union. In the decades that followed, the country underwent a significant transition toward democracy and sought closer economic and political integration with the European Union (EU) and NATO. This shift was marked by major public movements, most notably the Orange Revolution (2004) and the Euromaidan protests (2013\u20132014).\n\n### 4. The Russo-Ukrainian War\nIn 2014, following the Euromaidan protests, Russia illegally annexed the Crimean Peninsula and backed armed separatists in Ukraine\u2019s eastern Donbas region. \n\nIn February 2022, Russia launched a full-scale, unprovoked invasion of Ukraine. This escalation triggered the largest armed conflict in Europe since World War II. \n* **The Current Frontline:** The war has settled into a highly intense conflict of attrition. Russian forces occupy roughly 20% of Ukrainian territory, primarily in Crimea and the eastern/southern regions of Donetsk, Luhansk, Zaporizhzhia, and Kherson. \n* **The Resistance:** Backed by military and financial aid from the United States, the European Union, and other global allies, Ukrainian forces continue to defend their territory, launch counter-offensives, and conduct long-range drone and missile strikes to disrupt Russian military logistics.\n* **Humanitarian Impact:** The war has caused a massive humanitarian crisis, displacing millions of Ukrainians internally and as refugees across Europe and the globe. Despite the ongoing war, Ukraine was officially granted EU candidate status, signaling its long-term path toward European integration.",
